              ]
            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the hospital course of your patient, a 27-year-old male, admitted on 14/02/1997 with a two-day history of right iliac fossa pain, nausea, and low-grade fever, clinically consistent with acute appendicitis. On admission, physical examination revealed rebound tenderness at McBurney's point. Blood tests confirmed leukocytosis with left shift. Abdominal ultrasonography supported the diagnosis of acute appendicitis without evidence of perforation. The patient was resuscitated with intravenous fluids and commenced on analgesic and antibiotic therapy. On 15/02/1997, he successfully underwent laparoscopic appendectomy. The postoperative course was uneventful. Wound care was performed daily, vital signs remained stable, and the patient was mobilised early with physiotherapy support. He was discharged home on 18/02/1997 in good general condition. Please ensure follow-up within two weeks. Kind regards.</div>"
            },
